    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "From a strictly international law perspective, legitimacy is largely overlooked because both groups are recognized as sovereign peoples with equally valid claims to self-determination under treaties and the UN Charter. While the 1922 British Mandate and subsequent 1947 UN Partition Plan attempted to define borders, neither the Zionist founding of Israel in 1948 nor the Palestinian occupation in 1967 constitutes a clear victory or defeat in terms of \"right to return\" under international law; rather, the conflict remains a case of competing national rights. Consequently, historians generally avoid declaring one side's claim more \"legit\" than the other, focusing instead on analyzing how each side was justified within the legal frameworks of their time.",
